== Argument

#smallcaps[The] First Book deals with the knowledge of God the Creator. But since it is in the creation of man that the divine perfections are best displayed, man too is made a subject of discussion. So the whole book falls into two main heads—the first concerning the knowledge of God, the second the knowledge of man. In the first chapter these are considered together; in each of the following chapters, separately, though at times mixed with other matters that belong to one head or the other: for example, the discussions of Scripture and of images, which fall under the first head, and the other three, on the creation of the world, the holy angels, and devils, which fall under the second. The last point discussed—namely, the manner of the divine government—relates to both.

On the first head—namely, the knowledge of God—it is shown, first, what kind of knowledge God requires, Chapter 2. Second (Chapters 3–9), it is shown where this knowledge must be sought: not in man, because, although it is naturally implanted in the human mind, it is stifled, partly by ignorance, partly by evil intent, Chapters 3 and 4; not in the frame of the world, because, although it shines most clearly there, we are so dull that these displays of it, however plain, pass away without any benefit, Chapter 5; but in Scripture (Chapter 6), which is dealt with in Chapters 7–9. Third, it is shown what God is like, Chapter 10. Fourth, it is shown how impious it is to give God a visible form (here images, their worship, and its origin are considered), Chapter 11. Fifth, it is shown that God alone is to be worshiped, and worshiped wholly, Chapter 12. Lastly, Chapter 13 deals with the unity of the divine essence and the distinction of three persons.

On the second head—namely, the knowledge of man—Chapter 14 deals first with the creation of the world and with good and bad angels (all of which relate to man). Then Chapter 15, taking up man himself, examines his nature and his powers.

To shed more light on the nature of both God and man, the three remaining chapters—namely, 16-18—go on to deal with the general government of the world, and particularly of human actions, as against fortune and fate, explaining both the doctrine and its use. In conclusion, it is shown that though God uses the wicked as his instruments, he is pure from sin and from every kind of taint.
